<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Table scene for the empowerment demo.

  Contains a table surface, a pushable puck, and an overhead depth camera
  looking straight down at the puck region.

  The camera is deliberately a separate static model rather than wrist
  mounted. A wrist camera moves with the arm, so every action changes the
  whole image through ego-motion, and the empowerment estimate saturates:
  everything looks distinguishable because everything moved. A fixed
  overhead view isolates what the arm actually changed in the world.

  The puck's mass and friction are the analogue of the paper's
  pushable/immovable box conditions. To run the control condition, either
  set <static>true</static> on the puck or launch with bolted:=0.0, which
  changes the agent's internal model to match.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="empowerment_table">

    <physics name="1ms" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-contact-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Contact"/>

    <!--
      Lighting. There was no <scene> block here at all, so gz-sim used its
      default ambient, which is dark, and a single shadow-casting sun did the
      rest of the work. Worse, the old sun pointed along -x, which is
      straight from the robot toward the bench, so the arm threw its own
      shadow across exactly the region the camera is watching.

      Now: a real ambient term, the sun turned round to +x so shadows fall
      away from the bench, and a shadowless fill from the opposite side to
      lift what is left. None of this touches the depth image, which is
      geometry rather than light; it is for the GUI and for anyone filming
      the thing.
    -->
    <scene>
      <ambient>0.55 0.55 0.58 1</ambient>
      <background>0.75 0.80 0.86 1</background>
      <shadows>true</shadows>
    </scene>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 10 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.85 0.85 0.82 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
      <direction>0.45 0.25 -0.86</direction>
    </light>

    <light type="directional" name="fill">
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 6 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.45 0.45 0.50 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.6 -0.35 -0.72</direction>
    </light>

    <!-- Ground -->
    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>10 10</size></plane></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><plane><normal>0 0 1</normal><size>10 10</size></plane></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- =================================================================
      Layout. Both tables the same height, tops at z = 0.75:

        robot_table    0.50 x 0.55, centred (0.10, 0)   x from -0.15 to 0.35
        object_table   0.60 x 0.80, centred (-0.75, 0)  x from -1.05 to -0.45

      0.30 m of clear air between their near edges.

      The UR5 does not bolt straight to its table. It sits on robot_riser, a
      0.25 m block, so the arm base is at z = 1.00 while the work
      surface stays at 0.75. That riser is not decoration, and the tables
      cannot simply be raised to meet it:

        SDF has no way to state initial joint positions, so Gazebo always
        spawns the arm at all zeros, and a UR5 at all zeros is stretched
        horizontally 0.817 m out with the wrist 5 mm BELOW its own base
        plane. Bolt it level with a bench that is within 0.87 m and the arm
        materialises inside the bench, jams, and then creeps toward its
        target over minutes while shoving the puck around. Moving the bench
        outside that radius would put it outside the arm's 0.85 m reach, so
        clearance has to come from height. Putting it in the robot's mount
        instead of the tables keeps both tables level, which is what the
        scene should look like anyway.

      The UR5 is also yawed 180 degrees so it faces the object table.
      intrinsic_core's DH convention is 180 degrees about z from
      ur_description's URDF: at identical joint angles the analytic model
      puts the tool at (-0.631, -0.109) and Gazebo puts it at
      (+0.631, +0.109). Without the yaw the simulated arm reaches away from
      the bench while the agent plans toward it. See BASE_YAW in
      launch/gazebo_ur5.launch.py.

      The agent's frame has its origin at the arm base, so the work surface
      is at z = -0.25 there. That is table_height in
      config/empowerment.yaml and it must equal 0.75 minus 1.00.
      ================================================================= -->
    <!-- Robot table. -->
    <model name="robot_table">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0.1 0 0.730 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="top_collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.5 0.55 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.4</mu><mu2>0.4</mu2></ode></friction></surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="top_visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.5 0.55 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.7 0.66 0.6 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.8 0.75 0.68 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg0_collision">
          <pose>0.190 0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg0_visual">
          <pose>0.190 0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg1_collision">
          <pose>0.190 -0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg1_visual">
          <pose>0.190 -0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg2_collision">
          <pose>-0.190 0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg2_visual">
          <pose>-0.190 0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg3_collision">
          <pose>-0.190 -0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg3_visual">
          <pose>-0.190 -0.215 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Mounting riser. The UR5 bolts to the top of this, at z = 1.00. -->
    <model name="robot_riser">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 0 0.875 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.16 0.16 0.25</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.16 0.16 0.25</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.18 0.20 0.24 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.24 0.27 0.32 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Workbench. Objects live here, camera looks straight down at it. -->
    <model name="object_table">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-0.75 0 0.730 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="top_collision">
          <geometry><box><size>0.6 0.8 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.4</mu><mu2>0.4</mu2></ode></friction></surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="top_visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.6 0.8 0.04</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.7 0.66 0.6 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.8 0.75 0.68 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg0_collision">
          <pose>0.240 0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg0_visual">
          <pose>0.240 0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg1_collision">
          <pose>0.240 -0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg1_visual">
          <pose>0.240 -0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg2_collision">
          <pose>-0.240 0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg2_visual">
          <pose>-0.240 0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <collision name="leg3_collision">
          <pose>-0.240 -0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="leg3_visual">
          <pose>-0.240 -0.340 -0.375 0 0 0</pose>
          <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.710</size></box></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.32 0.32 0.35 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!--
      The puck. 0.03 m radius, i.e. 60 mm across, and the size is chosen for
      the GRIPPER rather than for the empowerment map. The 2F-85 opens to
      85 mm, so an 80 mm object leaves 2.5 mm of clearance per finger against
      a detector accurate to about 6 mm: the fingers hit the object instead of
      straddling it, and the descent stalls short every time. 60 mm gives
      12.5 mm a side, which the perception can actually hit.

      Light and low-friction so the arm can actually displace it;
      a heavy puck makes the empowerment contrast vanish, which is the
      bolted-box condition arriving by the back door.
    -->
    <model name="puck">
      <pose>-0.68 0.0 0.79 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <inertial>
          <mass>0.05</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>3.2e-5</ixx><iyy>3.2e-5</iyy><izz>4.0e-5</izz>
            <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.25</mu><mu2>0.25</mu2></ode></friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.7 0.15 0.15 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.9 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-pose-publisher-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::PosePublisher">
        <publish_link_pose>false</publish_link_pose>
        <publish_model_pose>true</publish_model_pose>
        <!-- false => one gz.msgs.Pose, which is what config/gz_bridge.yaml
             maps to geometry_msgs/PoseStamped. true would emit Pose_V and
             the bridge would silently carry nothing. -->
        <use_pose_vector_msg>false</use_pose_vector_msg>
        <update_frequency>30</update_frequency>
      </plugin>
    </model>

    <!--
      ========================================================================
      The contingency triad.

      Three objects, deliberately IDENTICAL to the depth camera: same
      cylinder, same 0.04 m radius, same 0.08 m height, same position in x,
      spread only in y. The colours differ so a human can follow what is
      happening; nothing the agent senses can tell them apart. They differ
      only in how they respond to being touched:

        puck         moves when the arm pushes it        contingent
        fixed_block  never moves                         unresponsive
        drifter      moves on its own, on a fixed cycle  non-contingent

      That third one is the interesting object and the reason this scene
      exists. It is a "noisy TV": something that changes constantly but not
      because of anything the agent did. A curiosity or prediction-error
      agent is drawn to it precisely because it is unpredictable, and gets
      stuck watching it. Empowerment should ignore it, because the mutual
      information between the AGENT'S ACTIONS and its state is zero: it
      moves, but not for you.

      So the experiment discriminates between two intrinsic objectives
      rather than merely demonstrating one:

                      static    drifter    puck
        curiosity      low      HIGH       medium
        empowerment    low      low        HIGH

      Spread +-0.24 m in y. That keeps all three inside the camera's 50 deg
      view (0.89 m across at this range) and inside the arm's 0.85 m reach
      (0.75 m to the outer two).
      ========================================================================
    -->

    <!--
      Unresponsive. <static> makes it immovable by any force, which is the
      paper's bolted-box control condition realised in the simulator rather
      than in the agent's model. Note that bolted:=0.0 does something
      different and complementary: it lies to the AGENT about the puck while
      the simulator still lets it move.
    -->
    <model name="fixed_block">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>-0.68 0.24 0.79 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.18 0.20 0.26 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.25 0.28 0.36 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-pose-publisher-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::PosePublisher">
        <publish_link_pose>false</publish_link_pose>
        <publish_model_pose>true</publish_model_pose>
        <use_pose_vector_msg>false</use_pose_vector_msg>
        <update_frequency>30</update_frequency>
      </plugin>
    </model>

    <!--
      Non-contingent motion. Slides back and forth along y on a prismatic
      joint, driven by intrinsic_motivation_ros/drifter_driver, which
      republishes a looping trajectory. Nothing the arm does affects it.

      Built as anchor + block rather than as a free body because SDF gives
      no way to script a pose over time: a joint plus the same
      JointTrajectoryController already driving the arm needs no new plugin
      and no physics of its own. The anchor is massless-looking but must
      carry inertia, since sdformat rejects a dynamic link without it, and
      is pinned to the world by a fixed joint so the pair does not fall.

      Damping on the joint keeps it from ringing when the trajectory
      reverses; the block rests on the table, so the controller also has to
      overcome about 0.2 N of friction, which is nothing.
    -->
    <model name="drifter">
      <!--
        3 mm higher than the other two objects, and deliberately so. Resting
        the block on the table while a prismatic joint also constrains it is
        a redundant constraint: the contact stick-slips against the joint's
        controller and the block jitters instead of sliding. Measured resting
        on the table, it reached only +-0.06 m of its +-0.10 m setpoint and
        showed speeds up to 2.9 m/s on a joint limited to 0.5.

        The joint carries its weight, so the clearance costs nothing, and
        3 mm is far below anything the detector distinguishes: all three
        objects still come back at the same standoff to within a centimetre.
      -->
      <pose>-0.68 -0.24 0.793 0 0 0</pose>

      <link name="anchor">
        <inertial>
          <mass>1.0</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>1e-3</ixx><iyy>1e-3</iyy><izz>1e-3</izz>
            <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
      </link>
      <joint name="anchor_to_world" type="fixed">
        <parent>world</parent>
        <child>anchor</child>
      </joint>

      <link name="block">
        <inertial>
          <mass>0.05</mass>
          <inertia>
            <ixx>3.2e-5</ixx><iyy>3.2e-5</iyy><izz>4.0e-5</izz>
            <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz>
          </inertia>
        </inertial>
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <surface>
            <friction><ode><mu>0.25</mu><mu2>0.25</mu2></ode></friction>
          </surface>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
          <material>
            <ambient>0.15 0.45 0.20 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.20 0.65 0.28 1</diffuse>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>

      <joint name="slide" type="prismatic">
        <parent>anchor</parent>
        <child>block</child>
        <axis>
          <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
          <limit><lower>-0.12</lower><upper>0.12</upper><effort>50</effort><velocity>0.5</velocity></limit>
          <dynamics><damping>3.0</damping></dynamics>
        </axis>
      </joint>

      <!--
        Position controller, not the JointTrajectoryController the arm uses.

        The trajectory controller was the obvious choice and it does not work
        here. Its semantics are "replace the current trajectory", and it
        warns on every replacement; driving a continuous oscillation means
        republishing constantly, and each replacement restarted the motion
        against a stale start time so the joint snapped straight to a point
        most of a cycle away. Measured: 0.196 m of travel between two samples
        25 ms apart, which is 7.8 m/s on a joint limited to 0.5 m/s, and it
        broke the detector's tracker into four tracks for three objects
        because the block jumped further between frames than the association
        gate.

        A position controller takes one scalar setpoint per message and PIDs
        toward it, so a driver publishing a sine at 20 Hz produces smooth
        motion with no replacement semantics anywhere.

        Gains are for a 0.05 kg block against 3.0 N s/m of joint damping,
        overdamped so it tracks the setpoint without ringing. The force limit
        does not set the speed here: the setpoint does, and it is a slow
        sine, so there is enough authority to actually follow it rather than
        lag at two thirds amplitude.
      -->
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
        <topic>/drifter/position</topic>
        <joint_name>slide</joint_name>
        <p_gain>200</p_gain>
        <i_gain>5</i_gain>
        <d_gain>20</d_gain>
        <i_min>-5</i_min>
        <i_max>5</i_max>
        <cmd_min>-20</cmd_min>
        <cmd_max>20</cmd_max>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Ground truth for the drifter, for evaluating the detector only; the
        agent never subscribes to it. Joint state rather than pose: the
        model's root is the anchor, which never moves, and the PosePublisher
        would have to emit both links on one topic with no way to tell them
        apart. The slide joint's position is unambiguous, and the block's
        world y is just the model pose's y plus that value.
      -->
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
        <topic>/drifter/joint_state</topic>
      </plugin>
    </model>

    <!--
      Overhead depth camera.

      640x480 is what a RealSense D435 publishes, but the empowerment node
      downsamples hard before use (see camera_width/camera_height in
      config/empowerment.yaml). That downsampling IS the sensor model: at
      full resolution every rollout differs in some pixel, nothing ever
      collapses, and empowerment saturates everywhere.
    -->
    <model name="overhead_camera">
      <static>true</static>
      <!--
        Oblique, not overhead. Two reasons, both load bearing.

        Occlusion. Looking straight down from directly above the puck, the
        arm covers the puck completely at exactly the configuration where
        the agent is doing something interesting: reaching to the puck's
        (x, y) puts the arm between camera and object, and zero pixels
        differ between a scene with the puck and one without. Anything that
        locates the object from the depth image therefore loses it at
        contact. The arm approaches from +x, so the camera is placed at -x
        and looks back toward it; the object is then in front of the arm
        from the camera's point of view rather than behind it.

        Lift is invisible from overhead. Raising the puck 0.1 m changes its
        depth reading from 0.87 to 0.77 and makes the blob slightly larger,
        and nothing else. From this angle a lifted object separates from the
        table and its height above the surface is directly measurable, which
        matters as soon as picking something up is the goal.

        Aimed at the puck at (-0.68, 0, 0.79): direction (0.77, 0, -0.56),
        so pitch is atan(0.56/0.77) = 0.6283 rad, 36 deg below horizontal,
        yaw 0 to look back along +x. Range to target is 0.952 m, within a
        millimetre of the 0.95 m the old overhead pose had, so depth range
        and the footprint of one pixel at the table are unchanged and the
        sensor-resolution numbers in the README stay comparable.

        The name is now a lie. Kept anyway: it appears in the gz topic
        names, in config/gz_bridge.yaml and in the RViz config, and renaming
        it buys nothing.
      -->
      <pose>-1.45 0 1.35 0 0.6283 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>0.05 0.09 0.03</size></box></geometry>
          <material><diffuse>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
        <sensor name="depth_camera" type="depth_camera">
          <always_on>1</always_on>
          <!--
            5 Hz, not 30. The empowerment node consumes frames at about 1 Hz,
            and a 640x480 32FC1 image is 1.2 MB, so 30 Hz is 37 MB/s of
            deserialisation into a single-threaded executor that also has to
            run the estimator. Measured at 30 Hz the node managed 9 ticks in
            110 s, an effective 0.08 Hz, and the resulting long gap between
            detections let the moving object cross the tracker's association
            gate and split into two tracks. The symptom looked like a
            perception bug and was a throughput one.
          -->
          <update_rate>5</update_rate>
          <visualize>true</visualize>
          <topic>depth_camera</topic>
          <!--
            gz_frame_id overrides the default frame_id
            (overhead_camera::link::depth_camera), which RViz cannot resolve.
            launch/gazebo_ur5.launch.py publishes base_link -> this frame.

            horizontal_fov is 50 deg, and must stay equal to camera_fov_deg
            in config/empowerment.yaml. The analytic camera the agent
            predicts with is only honest if it has the same optics as the one
            it actually senses through.

            Widened from 30 deg with the move to three objects: at 0.95 m
            range, 30 deg spans 0.51 m across the image and the outer two
            objects fall outside it. 50 deg spans 0.89 m, which covers the
            table's 0.8 m width. The README's measured resolution figures
            were taken at 30 deg looking straight down and do not carry over
            unchanged; re-run scripts/sweep_parameters.py before trusting
            any number that depends on them.
          -->
          <gz_frame_id>depth_camera_link</gz_frame_id>
          <camera>
            <horizontal_fov>0.8727</horizontal_fov>
            <image>
              <width>640</width>
              <height>480</height>
              <format>R_FLOAT32</format>
            </image>
            <clip>
              <near>0.1</near>
              <far>2.5</far>
            </clip>
            <noise>
              <type>gaussian</type>
              <mean>0.0</mean>
              <stddev>0.003</stddev>
            </noise>
          </camera>
        </sensor>
      </link>
    </model>

  </world>
</sdf>
